  "text": "Question eight. What is the difference between SGD, Momentum, RMSProp, and Adam? Stochastic gradient descent steps against each mini-batch's gradient. Momentum keeps a decaying average of past gradients, which damps the zigzag. RMSProp divides each parameter's step by the root of a running average of its squared gradients; Adam does both. Give two parameters gradients a hundred times apart: SGD's steps are a hundred and four times apart, Adam's, one point oh three. The follow-up is memory. In thirty-two-bit floats, a weight and its gradient take eight bytes; momentum adds four, Adam eight. A billion weights need eight gigabytes with SGD and sixteen with Adam, before any activations. Adam is the usual default; well-tuned SGD with momentum often generalizes slightly better on vision, in a quarter less memory. A network that trains well can still memorize.",
